WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.345
Lesson 5.5.

00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:05.465
Smart-contract risk & what audits don't prove.

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:07.491
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.491 --> 00:00:10.064
able to weigh contract risk honestly, and read

00:00:10.064 --> 00:00:12.090
an audit for what it actually covers.

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:14.680
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:16.365
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.365 --> 00:00:19.080
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:20.605
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.605 --> 00:00:22.767
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.067 --> 00:00:27.899
First, Common failure types: logic bugs, reentrancy (a

00:00:27.899 --> 00:00:31.105
contract is called back before it updates its

00:00:31.105 --> 00:00:35.425
state), broken access control, and economic exploits (correct

00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:38.582
code, exploitable incentives or oracles).

00:00:38.582 --> 00:00:42.387
Next, Audits review specific code at a specific time.

00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:46.059
Check the scope (which contracts), date (before later changes?),

00:00:46.059 --> 00:00:49.127
severity of findings and whether they were fixed.

00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:51.052
Then, Bug bounties pay hackers

00:00:51.052 --> 00:00:53.152
to report instead of exploit.

00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:55.987
A large, active bounty is a good sign.

00:00:55.987 --> 00:00:58.502
And finally, Time and value at risk:

00:00:58.502 --> 00:01:01.085
code that has safely held large value

00:01:01.085 --> 00:01:03.872
for years has survived more real attacks.

00:01:05.172 --> 00:01:07.687
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:07.687 --> 00:01:12.177
Protocol X: two audits, but the latest upgrade (last

00:01:12.177 --> 00:01:16.967
month) isn't in either scope; bounty $50k; TVL $400M.

00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:19.123
The newest code is unaudited and the

00:01:19.123 --> 00:01:21.637
bounty is small relative to the value.

00:01:21.637 --> 00:01:23.002
Size down, or wait.

00:01:24.302 --> 00:01:25.762
Here's your checklist.

00:01:25.762 --> 00:01:27.297
Do it now, for real.

00:01:27.297 --> 00:01:29.574
First, Audit scope matches deployed

00:01:29.574 --> 00:01:31.772
code; date after last upgrade.

00:01:31.772 --> 00:01:34.907
Next, Critical/high findings resolved.

00:01:34.907 --> 00:01:38.832
And finally, Bug bounty size relative to TVL checked.

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:41.342
Question 1.

00:01:41.342 --> 00:01:42.847
What does an audit not prove?

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.575
The answer: That the code (or later

00:01:49.575 --> 00:01:51.947
changes, or economic design) is safe.

00:01:53.247 --> 00:01:54.492
Question 2.

00:01:54.492 --> 00:01:55.677
What is reentrancy?

00:02:00.097 --> 00:02:03.088
The answer: A contract being called back before it

00:02:03.088 --> 00:02:06.842
finishes updating its state, letting an attacker repeat actions.

00:02:08.142 --> 00:02:09.412
Question 3.

00:02:09.412 --> 00:02:10.767
Why check audit dates?

00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:18.507
The answer: Code changed after the audit wasn't reviewed.

00:02:19.807 --> 00:02:21.052
Let's recap.

00:02:21.052 --> 00:02:24.420
Common failure types: logic bugs, reentrancy (a

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.368
contract is called back before it updates

00:02:27.368 --> 00:02:30.736
its state), broken access control, and economic

00:02:30.736 --> 00:02:35.176
exploits (correct code, exploitable incentives or oracles).

00:02:35.176 --> 00:02:38.346
Audits review specific code at a specific time.

00:02:38.346 --> 00:02:41.821
Bug bounties pay hackers to report instead of exploit.

00:02:41.821 --> 00:02:44.138
Time and value at risk: code that

00:02:44.138 --> 00:02:46.727
has safely held large value for years

00:02:46.727 --> 00:02:48.840
has survived more real attacks.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.655
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:52.655 --> 00:02:55.922
Next up, Lesson 5.6: Beyond Ethereum:

00:02:55.922 --> 00:02:59.104
Solana, Bitcoin and other ecosystems.
